A||||2||FieroPerformance||Exhaust falling apart... What to do?||||1||
Z||000000||FieroPerformance||10-30-2001||07:19 PM||||I am planning on buying a brand new exhaust from Rodney when his is ready for sale. However, until then I've got a rusty exhaust on my hands that is falling apart. As you can see from the picture below, the left pipe running from the muffler to the tip has already fallen off (while driving I might add). The other problem is that the muffler is turning on the pipe that runs from the cat to it. This is causing my tips (tip) to hang down from the car and look funny. The broken off pipe has caused the back of my car to get very dirty with a black grime, however I don't think this will be a problem once my rebuild is done.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/engineexhaust.jpg"> <P>The options that I can think of are:<BR>1. try to support the muffler by either welding or hanging it, then saw off the left pipe where it comes out of the muffler and cap off the hole (turning my exhaust into a single exit system)<P>2. Cut the pipe that runs from the cat right before it comes to the muffler. Thus running with no muffler and no tips. Would this make the back of my car dirty even with a VERY clean running engine?<P>Does anyone have any other ideas? This only has to last for a few months and then I plan on getting a whole new exhaust from Rodney.<P>Thanks!<BR>-Charlie<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroPerformance||10-30-2001||09:58 PM||||*bump* to get answer and to see my new signature  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>-Charlie<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsigfperformance.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||tgowens||10-31-2001||02:16 AM||||Charlie,<P>My opinions are this:<P>1. No. You really don't want to change the characteristics of the exhaust system by changing the back-flow of the system. This will affect engine performance.<P>2. No. running with a cat only also will change the characteristics of the exhaust system. This configuration can also cause an abnormally high amount of heat under your engine.<P>I recently drove an '87GT 2.8 which had a clogged cat and a muffler with a split seam. The result was so much heat coming from the cat through the muffler that it caught the rear of the cradle on fire and burned out the rear transmission mount.<P>Do yourself a favor and at least have the system fixed in order to preserve the engine after you reinstall it. You'll be glad you did!||||reg||1
